#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183949 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184146 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dce948b0-e63a-5dcc-a33a-e68492b98321" :knowledge-point/title "Mixed Numbers by Proper Fractions"}] :question/id #uuid "71291025-e9b3-55b1-9de2-1e6be8310476" :question/math-academy-id "e-14571" :question/problem "A field has an area of $2 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}$ acres. If a farmer wants to use $\\dfrac{3}{5}$ of the field to grow corn, how much of the field would be used to grow corn?" :question/worked-solution "To find out the area used to grow corn, we need to calculate\n\n$$\n2 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3} \\times \\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write\n\n$$\n2 \\,\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2 \\,\\dfrac{2}{3} = \\dfrac{(2 \\times 3) + 2}{3} = \\dfrac{8}{3}\n$$\n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{\\color{red}3} \\times \\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{5}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's denominator ($\\color{red}3$) and the second fraction's numerator ($\\color{blue}3$) have a common factor of $3$.\n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{8}{3} \\cdot \\frac{3}{5} &= \\frac{8}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{8}{5} \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{8}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by converting this fraction to a mixed number, we get\n\n$$\n8\\div 5 = 1\\,\\textrm{R} \\, 3 = 1\\,\\dfrac{3}{5} \\,\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nacres would be used to grow corn."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183950 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184147 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b88656f-353a-5d5a-88f7-c85a5c9eec3e" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Mixed Numbers"}] :question/id #uuid "22c9774c-400e-5baf-a874-fda211b2bb61" :question/math-academy-id "e-14572" :question/problem "A chocolate bar is made with $1\\, \\dfrac{1}{4}\\,\\textrm{oz}$ of cocoa. If Casey has $2\\, \\dfrac{2}{3}$ bars, how many ounces of cocoa did it take to make them?" :question/worked-solution "To find out how many ounces of cocoa it took to make the chocolate bars, we need to calculate\n\n$$\n1 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4} \\times 2 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4} = \\dfrac{(1 \\times 4) + 1}{4}= \\dfrac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nWe also write\n\n$$\n2 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}=\\dfrac{(2 \\times 3)+2}{3}= \\dfrac{8}{3}\n$$\n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{\\color{red}4} \\times \\dfrac{\\color{blue}8}{3}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's denominator ($\\color{red}4$) and the second fraction's numerator ($\\color{blue}8$) have a common factor of $4$.\n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5}{4} \\cdot \\frac{8}{3} &= \\frac{5}{3} \\cdot \\frac{8}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{3} \\cdot \\frac{2}{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5 \\cdot 2}{3 \\cdot 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{10}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{10}{3} =3\\,\\textrm{R}\\, 1 = 3 \\, \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nSo, it took\n\n$$\n3 \\, \\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\\textrm{oz}\n$$\n\nof cocoa to make Casey's chocolate bars."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183951 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184145 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b3a2302-d863-584a-acd6-1025ff362a04" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Mixed Numbers by Whole Numbers"}] :question/id #uuid "5a6ee672-4f62-5fc1-8285-b30ba76e955d" :question/math-academy-id "e-14573" :question/problem "Customers are served every $2\\,\\dfrac{1}{4}$ minutes at a particular bank. How long does it take to serve eight customers?" :question/worked-solution "To find out how long it takes to serve eight customers, we need to multiply\n\n$$\n2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nby $8$.\n\nFirst, we write\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac{1}{4} = \\dfrac{(2 \\times 4) + 1}{4} = \\dfrac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nWe also write $8$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n8 = \\dfrac{8}{1}\n$$\n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9}{\\color{red}4} \\times \\dfrac{\\color{blue}8}{1}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's denominator ($\\color{red}4$) and the second fraction's numerator ($\\color{blue}8$) have a common\nfactor of $4$.\n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{9}{4} \\cdot \\frac{8}{1} &= \\frac{9}{1} \\cdot \\frac{8}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{9}{1} \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 9 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it takes $18$ minutes to serve $8$ customers."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184545 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184145 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b3a2302-d863-584a-acd6-1025ff362a04" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Mixed Numbers by Whole Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1ac377e-6f4e-54d4-b88e-05b466cb4f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12\\frac{1}{2}$ cups" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181447} {:answer/id #uuid "fdd4ed31-9be5-5d1e-bae0-a62c3f063741" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$13\\frac{1}{2}$ cups" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181448} {:answer/id #uuid "31fe2c32-3b9c-5e11-a992-a17c125d014c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$11\\frac{1}{2}$ cups" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181449} {:answer/id #uuid "485a8039-5f0f-5859-bc1c-496c2d38256e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10\\frac{